<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pietrabbondante</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pietrabbondante is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, beside the remains of the largest sanctuary of the ancient Samnites. Built mainly in the 2nd century BCE, the complex includes temples and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, and it served as a religious and political centre for the Samnite peoples. It fell out of use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-91-87-bce">Social War</a> of 91–88 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pietrabbondante.jpg" alt="Pietrabbondante" width="320" height="238" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stundra · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pietrabbondante.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pietrabbondante is a municipality in the Province of Isernia in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a>, about 30 kilometres north-west of Campobasso and about 20 kilometres north-east of Isernia. It lies at the base of towering rocks on a mountain whose slopes have yielded many megalithic remains. The name Petra Habundante was in use after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> of the 8th century, and the present name is explained by the abundance of limestone rocks and debris in the area.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known inhabitants were the Samnites, who arrived in the 6th century BCE. Many historians believe the site was home to the assemblies of the Samnite federal government. Earlier scholarship took it for the Samnite town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bovianum">Bovianum</a>, which is now thought to be wrong, and the site is viewed as a Samnite sanctuary. Use of it seems to have declined significantly after the defeat of the Samnites and their allies by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Lucius Cornelius Sulla</a> in the Social Wars. In 957 the town was the capital of one of the thirty-four counties into which the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a> was divided.</p><p>Excavations at the site were carried out by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a> in 1857 and 1858, later at the interest of the Province, and from 1959 by the Archaeological Superintendent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a>. They brought to light sections of marble statues, clay tools, coins and weapons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>On the slope of Monte Saraceno the Samnites built a complex of worship consisting of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a temple and two arcaded buildings, begun in the late 2nd century BCE and intended for both worship and institutional business. The theatre-temple complex stands about 966 metres above sea level on two terraces on a single axis, and its area measures 55 by 90 metres. The theatre, built of polygonal limestone and a rare example of such a structure by the Samnites, could hold up to 2,500 spectators, with each seat cut from a single block. It stands on the site of an earlier Ionic temple portico of the 3rd century BCE, which was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a> in 217 BCE.</p><p>The temple behind it measures 22 by 35 metres, and only its podium and the base of eight columns survive. An inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> records Statius Claro, who paid for half of the podium. The town is now a tourist site, and its church of Santa Maria Assunta has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> portal.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pietrabbondante” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great sanctuary at <b>Pietrabbondante</b>, with its temple and theatre, was a federal centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/samnite-league">Samnite League</a></li></ul></section>
